-- Capabilities widening, Task 1 -- a SECOND, independent subsumption shape:
-- a MID-TIER declared `needs` (not the root `IO`, not an exact match) covers
-- a deeper descendant. `IO.NetConnect`'s parent is `IO.Network`
-- (`cap_lattice.ml:22`); declaring `needs IO.Network` here covers
-- `Cap(IO.NetConnect)` via the same ancestor-walk `cap_subsumes` uses for
-- t46, but one level down the tree instead of from the root — proving
-- subsumption holds at every tier, not only "root covers everything."
-- (`Cap(IO.Random)`/`Cap(IO.Database)`/`Cap(IO.Spawn)`/`Cap(IO.Mut)`/
-- `Cap(IO.Telemetry)`/`Cap(IO.Foreign)`/`Cap(IO.NetConnect.TLS)` are valid
-- `needs` targets but are NOT valid `Cap(X)` TYPE arguments today — only 10
-- of the 18 hierarchy entries are registered in `builtin_types`
-- (`typecheck.ml:1858-1861`); see core-march-types.md §2.8.3 for the finding.)
-- See core-march-types.md §2.8.
mod Client do
  needs IO.Network

  fn connect(cap : Cap(IO.NetConnect), host : String) : String do
    let _ = cap
    host
  end
end
